// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-10-14 (Wednesday): Today's date as pattern
// ============================================================================
// Weekly rotation slot: the calendar date itself drives the pattern. Today
// (IST) is 2026-10-14 -- day 14, month 10 (October), year 2026.
//
// THE DERIVATION (this is the whole point of the day):
//   Recent Wednesdays each found a different arithmetic relationship between
//   day and month (digit sequence 08-26; clock rates 09-02; a hocket when
//   day==month 09-09; two coprime grid-lengths 09-16; per-token Euclidean
//   flutters when day was prime 09-23; a GCD-reduced 10:3 polyrhythm 09-30;
//   day/month fed straight into euclid(7,10) as a mask 10-07). Today's two
//   numbers don't invite another ratio or GCD trick cleanly (GCD(14,10)=2,
//   reducing to 7:5 -- the same device-family as 09-30/10-07 twice running).
//   Instead this week reads the two numbers in BINARY and uses the bits
//   themselves, directly, as a rhythmic gate -- a different kind of math
//   entirely from ratios/Euclid, not a relabelled repeat:
//     day   14 = 1110 (bits: 8 4 2 1 -> 1 1 1 0)
//     month 10 = 1010 (bits: 8 4 2 1 -> 1 0 1 0)
//   Each 4-bit string becomes a fixed 4-token onset/rest mask for that
//   voice -- 1 sounds a note, 0 is a literal rest ("~") -- held constant
//   across all four alternating phrases below, exactly the way 10-07 held
//   its euclid(7,10) mask constant while the phrase content varied.
//   Comparing the two masks bit-by-bit is the actual payoff: bits 1 and 3
//   (the "8" and "2" places) agree -- both voices sound together there.
//   Bit 4 (the "1" place) also agrees -- both rest together. Only bit 2
//   (the "4" place) disagrees: day has a 1, month a 0, so DAY alone sounds
//   a note right where MONTH is silent. Three of four bits lock the voices
//   together; the fourth is where the date's two numbers genuinely differ,
//   and that's the one spot the piece asks the ear to notice a solo voice
//   where it just heard both.
//
//   Structural tones reuse this project's established day/month-mod-7
//   mapping (honest reuse -- the mapping hasn't changed, see 09-16/09-30's
//   own note on this): day 14 mod 7 = 0 -> tonic anchors DAY's phrases;
//   month 10 mod 7 = 3 -> scale degree 3 anchors MONTH's phrases.
//
//   DAY (mask "1 1 1 ~"): four alternating phrases -- CALL rises in leaps
//   (0->4->7, root/fifth/octave, real 4th+3rd scale-step leaps) and ends
//   open on the octave; RESPONSE answers downward (4->2->0), resolving to
//   tonic; TENSION revisits the overshoot peaks with the widest leap in the
//   piece (7->11->4, octave up to a 5th-above-the-octave and back); and
//   RESOLUTION is CALL's exact retrograde (7->4->0), a real retrograde
//   device, not just "a calmer phrase."
//
//   MONTH (mask "<token> ~ <token> ~", sparse): four alternating phrases
//   anchored on degree 3 -- CALL rises (3->6), RESPONSE answers (0->3),
//   TENSION leaps down (6->2), and RESOLUTION is CALL's own retrograde
//   (6->3) -- the same retrograde device as DAY, applied independently to
//   the sparser voice too, a small deliberate symmetry between the two.
//
//   CYCLES = 25 = day-digit-sum (1+4=5) + month (10) + year-digit-sum
//   (2+0+2+6=10). setcpm(72) x 25 cycles / 60 = 20.8s.
//
// Scale: C minor -- fresh for this slot (last four Wednesdays used A minor,
// E minor, G minor, and D major); the bits are the idea, not the mode.
//
// NO DRUM KIT, deliberately, not by default: the two binary masks interlocking
// (agree / agree / disagree / agree) ARE this piece's audible pulse, read
// straight off the date's own digits -- a metric kit underneath would just
// restate a pulse that's already there, the reflexive-percussion failure
// MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md flags.
//
// Instrumentation, chosen for what's distinctive about THIS week's device
// (binary bits), not a template carried over: DAY gets `square` -- the
// harmonically sharp, "digital-sounding" waveform (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §1's
// sharp/abrasive row), appropriate for the voice literally reading bits.
// MONTH gets `triangle` -- pure/soft, deliberately contrasting timbre
// (§1-2's blend/contrast) so the two masks stay legible as separate voices
// even where they agree. DRONE stays `sine` -- tanpura convention, tonic
// and fifth, no automation, held still under both (§2.5, §3). Only DAY
// carries a light acid-distortion + filter sweep (the taste layer, per
// CREATIVE-DIRECTION.md); the sweep period (4 cycles) is the exact bit
// value the two masks disagree on, tying the one automated parameter in the
// piece back to the one bit that makes this week's date interesting.
//
// This is a DAILY quick clip, not a flagship set -- one continuous pattern,
// no alap/jor/jhala arc.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(72)
let CYCLES = 25 // day-digit-sum (5) + month (10) + year-digit-sum (10)

stack(
  // DAY -- mask fixed by 14's binary (1110): tokens 1-3 sound, token 4
  // rests. Four alternating phrases, anchored on tonic (day mod 7 = 0).
  // CALL rises in leaps and ends open; RESPONSE answers downward to tonic;
  // TENSION revisits the overshoot peaks with the widest leap; RESOLUTION
  // is CALL's exact retrograde.
  n("<[0 4 7 ~] [4 2 0 ~] [7 11 4 ~] [7 4 0 ~]>")
    .scale("C:minor")
    .sound("square")
    .attack(.006).decay(.11).sustain(0).release(.05)
    .distort(.14) // acid taste layer, kept light so the bit-mask stays legible
    .lpf(sine.range(500, 2400).slow(4)) // sweep period = the one bit the masks disagree on
    .gain(.32)
    .pan(.68)
    .room(1.1),

  // MONTH -- mask fixed by 10's binary (1010): token 1 and 3 sound, 2 and 4
  // rest -- the sparser voice. Anchored on degree 3 (month mod 7 = 3).
  // CALL rises, RESPONSE answers, TENSION leaps down, RESOLUTION is CALL's
  // own retrograde.
  n("<[3 ~ 6 ~] [0 ~ 3 ~] [6 ~ 2 ~] [6 ~ 3 ~]>")
    .scale("C:minor")
    .sound("triangle")
    .attack(.04).decay(.3).sustain(.15).release(.9)
    .gain(.27)
    .pan(.32)
    .room(2.6),

  // DRONE -- tanpura convention, tonic/fifth, held for the full piece with
  // no automation (the one moving filter this week lives on DAY instead).
  note("[c2,g2]/25")
    .sound("sine")
    .attack(1.4).release(2)
    .gain(.22)
    .pan(.5)
    .room(3.2)
)
